# Ship

When the user says **ship** (exact word as a standing abbreviation; also clear equivalents such as “ship this” / “ship it” when they clearly mean this ritual):

Run these steps in order. Stop on failure.

| Step | Action |
| ---- | --- |
| 1 | **Move to a new branch** — follow `move-to-new-branch.mdc` fully (patch bump + slug, create/check out, `SetActiveBranch`). Slug from the work being shipped. |
| 2 | **Commit** — follow the committing-changes-with-git protocol (status/diff/log, stage relevant files, HEREDOC message, never skip hooks). If there is nothing to commit, stop after step 1 and say so; do not empty-commit. |
| 3 | **Push** — `git push -u origin HEAD` with host permissions so the new branch tracks remote. Do not force-push. |

## Not this ritual

- **Going for Release** / prep for release — versionCode bump, AAB, store notes. Do not run that when they only said **ship**.
- Ordinary “commit” or “push” alone — do only what they asked; do not invent a new branch.
- **Move to a new branch** alone — branch only; no commit/push unless they also asked.

## Notes

- Prefer a short camelCase slug that names the change (example: `1.4.13-lumenGet`).
- Return the new branch name and remote tracking confirmation when done.
